    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d380">

                <pb facs="0522" n="510" xml:id="pg510"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.30/5–646: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of
                            State to the Political Adviser in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Atcheson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-05-09T19:00:00-04:00">May 9, 1946—7 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>299. Reurad C–60653 [201].<anchor n="23" xml:id="d380-f1"/> Support of USSR of
                    general principles quoted Paragraph 1, Appendix A, SWNCC 186/12<anchor n="24" xml:id="d380-f2"/> was contained in
                    letter to Secretary Byrnes signed Molotov dated October 19, 1945.<anchor n="25"
                        xml:id="d380-f3"/> On Oct 26, 1945 Dept
                    orally informed British and Chinese Ambassadors that “we had decided to destroy
                    all large vessels of the Jap Navy such as
                    battleships and cruisers as well as Jap
                    submarines and that destroyers and floating vessels with lesser tonnage would be
                    divided equally among the four powers, that is, with the USSR, UK, China and the
                        US”.<anchor n="26" xml:id="d380-f4"/></p>
                <p>As stated in paragraph 2, Appendix A, SWNNC 186/12 formal agreement was requested
                    of UK, USSR and China to procedure outlined in Dept’s communication quoted therein. Replies have been received
                    from the USSR and China as noted in paragraph 3. No formal reply has been
                    received from U.K.</p>
                <p>With regard to further developments in respect of subject matter of paragraph 3
                    of same Appendix, note quoted in Appendix C was communicated to Soviet Foreign
                    Office on April 5 [<hi rendition="#italic">6</hi>]. On May 4 Dept sent to Embassy Moscow for transmittal in
                    substance to Soviet Foreign Office as supplement<anchor n="27" xml:id="d380-f5"
                    /> to note delivered April 5 the text proposed by JCS in SWNCC 186/13, 22 April
                    1946, which paper it is assumed you possess.<anchor n="28" xml:id="d380-f6"
                    /></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Acheson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d380-f1" n="23" xml:id="d380-f1-content">
                <p>Dated May 6, not printed; it asked for information in regard to disposition of
                    Japanese combatant naval vessels, as the Soviet member of the Allied Council,
                    Lt. Gen. Kuzma Nikolayevich Derevyanko, had raised questions concerning the
                    matter (894.30/5–646).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d380-f2" n="24" xml:id="d380-f2-content">
                <p>March 21, not printed, but see SWN–4203, April 26, <ref target="#pg505">p.
                        505</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d380-f3" n="25" xml:id="d380-f3-content">
                <p>See telegram 2199, October 22, 1945, noon, from Moscow, <ref
                        target="frus1945v06#pg_906"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>,
                        1945, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">vi</hi>, p. 906</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d380-f4" n="26" xml:id="d380-f4-content">
                <p>See memorandum by the Director of the Office of Far Eastern Affairs, October 26,
                    1945, <ref target="frus1945v06#pg_996"><hi rendition="#italic">Foreign
                            Relations</hi>, 1945, vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">vi</hi>, p.
                        996</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d380-f5" n="27" xml:id="d380-f5-content">
                <p>The supplementary letter was dated May 8, according to telegram 1478, May 9, 9
                    a.m., from Moscow.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d380-f6" n="28" xml:id="d380-f6-content">
                <p>See quotation in SWN–4203, April 26, <ref target="#pg505">p. 505</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
